tor_rank\":6,\"text\":\"For example, if y = a AND b then b is an irrelevant variable only in the portion of the tree in which a=0\\n\\nBut we can use simple statistics to warn us that we might be overfitting.\\n\\nCopyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 71\\n\\nCopyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 72\\n\\n36\",\"heading_path\":[\"Avoiding overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:233\",\"rrf_score\":0.026144907723855092,\"rank\":3,\"bm25_score\":5.506363443265546,\"bm25_rank\":17,\"vector_distance\":0.5279292464256287,\"vector_rank\":16,\"text\":\"Example:\\n\\nSlide 92\\n\\nMachine Learning Datasets\\n\\nWhat is Classification?\\n\\nContingency Tables\\n\\nOLAP (Online Analytical Processing)\\n\\nWhat is Data Mining?\\n\\nSearching for High Information Gain\\n\\nLearning an unpruneddecision tree recursively\\n\\nTraining Set Error\\n\\nTest Set Error\\n\\nOverfitting\\n\\nAvoiding Overfitting\\n\\nInformation Gain of a real valued input\\n\\nBuilding Decision Trees with real Valued Inputs\\n\\nAndrew's homebrewed hack: Binary Categorical Splits Example Decision Trees Copyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 93\\n\\n46\\n\\nCopyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 94\\n\\nMachine Learning Datasets\\n\\nWhat is Classification?\\n\\nContingency Tables\\n\\nOLAP (Online Analytical Processing)\\n\\nWhat is Data Mining?\\n\\nSearching for High Information Gain\\n\\nLearning an unpruneddecision tree recursively\\n\\nTraining Set Error\\n\\nTest Set Error\\n\\nOverfitting\\n\\nAvoiding Overfitting\\n\\nInformation Gain of a real valued input\\n\\nBuilding Decision Trees with real valued Inputs\\n\\nAndrew's homebrewed hack: Binary Categorical Splits\\n\\nExample Decision Trees\",\"heading_path\":[\"Example:\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:89\",\"rrf_score\":0.02532051282051282,\"rank\":4,\"bm25_score\":5.4189398949467416,\"bm25_rank\":20,\"vector_distance\":0.5320706367492676,\"vector_rank\":18,\"text\":\"Copyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 30\\n\\nMachine Learning Datasets\\n\\nLearning an unpruneddecision tree recursively\\n\\nWhat is Classification? Contingency Tables OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) What is Data Mining? Searching for High Information Gain Training Set Error Test Set Error Overfitting Avoiding Overfitting\\n\\nInformation Gain of a real valued input\\n\\nBuilding Decision Trees with real Valued Inputs\\n\\nAndrew's homebrewed hack: Binary Categorical Splits\\n\\nExample Decision Trees\\n\\n15\",\"heading_path\":[\"Learning Decision Trees\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:164\",\"rrf_score\":0.01639344262295082,\"rank\":5,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.3345930576324463,\"vector_rank\":1,\"text\":\"Overfitting\",\"heading_path\":[\"Overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:86\",\"rrf_score\":0.01639344262295082,\"rank\":6,\"bm25_score\":8.527857479559787,\"bm25_rank\":1,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"·A Decision Tree is a tree-structured plan of a set of attributes to test in order to predict the output.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Learning Decision Trees\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:165\",\"rrf_score\":0.016129032258064516,\"rank\":7,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.34078866243362427,\"vector_rank\":2,\"text\":\"·Definition: If your machine learning algorithm fits noise (i.e. pays attention to parts of the data that are irrelevant) it is overfitting.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:206\",\"rrf_score\":0.016129032258064516,\"rank\":8,\"bm25_score\":8.189044968808027,\"bm25_rank\":2,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"3. Construct a decision tree in which each row of the truth table corresponds to one path through the decision tree.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Simple proof:\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:117\",\"rrf_score\":0.015873015873015872,\"rank\":9,\"bm25_score\":7.286611169768634,\"bm25_rank\":3,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"For what number of records does the decision tree's prediction disagree with the true value in the database?\",\"heading_path\":[\"Training Set Error\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:10\",\"rrf_score\":0.015625,\"rank\":10,\"bm25_score\":7.06573537700755,\"bm25_rank\":4,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"Today's lecture\",\"heading_path\":[\"Today's lecture\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:168\",\"rrf_score\":0.015625,\"rank\":11,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.415641725063324,\"vector_rank\":4,\"text\":\"Avoiding overfitting\",\"heading_path\":[\"Avoiding overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:134\",\"rrf_score\":0.015384615384615385,\"rank\":12,\"bm25_score\":6.503193723770157,\"bm25_rank\":5,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"·But once learned, we see how well the tree predicts that data.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Test Set Error\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:257\",\"rrf_score\":0.015384615384615385,\"rank\":13,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.417230486869812,\"vector_rank\":5,\"text\":\"· Bayesian Decision Trees can take a different approach to preventing overfitting\",\"heading_path\":[\"What we haven't discussed\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:180\",\"rrf_score\":0.015151515151515152,\"rank\":14,\"bm25_score\":6.492262008229843,\"bm25_rank\":6,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"·Build the full decision tree as before.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Using Chi-squared to avoid overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:166\",\"rrf_score\":0.014925373134328358,\"rank\":15,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.4715529680252075,\"vector_rank\":7,\"text\":\"·Fact (theoretical and empirical): If your machine learning algorithm is overfitting then it may perform less well on test set data.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:249\",\"rrf_score\":0.014925373134328358,\"rank\":16,\"bm25_score\":6.085284389803495,\"bm25_rank\":7,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"·The recursive algorithm for building an unpruned decision tree\",\"heading_path\":[\"What you should know\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:12\",\"rrf_score\":0.014705882352941176,\"rank\":17,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.5034105181694031,\"vector_rank\":8,\"text\":\"·Learning a decision tree classifier from data\",\"heading_path\":[\"Today's lecture\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:182\",\"rrf_score\":0.014492753623188406,\"rank\":18,\"bm25_score\":5.992082001682459,\"bm25_rank\":9,\"vector_distance\":null,\"vector_rank\":null,\"text\":\"·Beginning at the bottom of the tree, delete splits in which pchance> MaxPchance.\",\"heading_path\":[\"Using Chi-squared to avoid overfitting\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d0:child:85\",\"rrf_score\":0.014492753623188406,\"rank\":19,\"bm25_score\":null,\"bm25_rank\":null,\"vector_distance\":0.5046699047088623,\"vector_rank\":9,\"text\":\"Learning Decision Trees\",\"heading_path\":[\"Learning Decision Trees\"],\"scoring_parent_id\":null},{\"chunk_id\":\"cd6684c353e44c7c88e349fccb10f8d