nce?\\\", \\\"requirements\\\": [{\\\"requirement_id\\\": \\\"R1\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"How the decision-tree lecture defines overfitting.\\\"}, {\\\"requirement_id\\\": \\\"R2\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"What effect overfitting can have on test-set performance according to the decision-tree lecture.\\\"}], \\\"context\\\": \\\"[1]\\\\nSource metadata: {\\\\\\\"file_name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"decision-trees.pdf\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"heading_path\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"Overfitting\\\\\\\"], \\\\\\\"document_labels\\\\\\\": [\\\\\\\"decision-trees\\\\\\\"]}\\\\nExcerpt:\\\\n[p1] Overfitting\\\\n\\\\n[p2] ·Definition: If your machine learning algorithm fits noise (i.e. pays attention to parts of the data that are irrelevant) it is overfitting.\\\\n\\\\n[p3] ·Fact (theoretical and empirical): If your machine learning algorithm is overfitting then it may perform less well on test set data.\\\\n\\\\n[p4] Copyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 69\\\\n\\\\n[p5] Copyright ©Andrew W. Moore Slide 70\\\\n\\\\n[p6] Machine Learning Datasets\\\\n\\\\n[p7] What is Classification?\\\\n\\\\n[p8] Contingency Tables\\\\n\\\\n[p9] OLAP (Online Analytical Processing)\\\\n\\\\n[p10] What is Data Mining?\\\\n\\\\n[p11] Searching for High Information Gain\\\\n\\\\n[p12] Learning an unpruneddecision tree recursively\\\\n\\\\n[p13] Training Set Error\\\\n\\\\n[p14] Test Set Error\\\\n\\\\n[p15] Overfitting\\\\n\\\\n[p16] Avoiding Overfitting\\\\n\\\\n[p17] Information Gain of a real valued input Building Decision Trees with real Valued Inputs Andrew's homebrewed hack: Binary Categorical Splits Example Decision Trees\\\\n\\\\n[p18] 35\\\\nPDF physical pages: 35\\\\nExcerpt canonical offsets: 16989:17804\\\\nExtraction limitations: OCR, visual figure/chart interpretation, and formula understanding are disabled. Reading order, spacing and table structure are parser estimates; review the original pages. Printed page labels are not extracted; page indices refer to physical PDF pages.\\\\n\\\\n[2]\\\\nSource 